## Routing — read state from the program, never from the conversation

`doc-agent-ai` decides where a node stands. You decide how to talk to the user
about it. Before delegating any phase, and after every phase returns, ask:

```
doc-agent-ai status --node <node>
```

### Route only on the typed answer

- **`nextRecommended`** names the phase to run. Delegate to that phase's sub-agent
  and no other.
- **`blockedReasons`** must be empty before you advance. While it is non-empty, the
  work in `nextRecommended` is unfinished — resume it, do not move on.
- **`nextAction.kind`** tells you what kind of step it is:
  - `start-phase` — the phase has no recorded work; delegate it.
  - `resume-phase` — the phase has gaps; delegate it again with the gaps named.
  - `decide-optional-phase` — ask the user before running it (see below).
  - `complete` — every applicable phase is done; there is nothing to delegate.
  - `cannot-determine` — the program could not decide. Its `command` is `null` on
    purpose. Report `reason` and `operatorHint` to the user and stop. Do not guess
    a next step, and do not invent a command.
- **`nextAction.command`** is runnable when present. Prefer it over composing your
  own.

Never derive the current phase from what was discussed, from which files you
believe exist, or from a checkbox in an index. Those are the failure modes this
routing rule exists to remove.

### Reporting progress between phases

Summarise from the status output, not from memory. `phases[].state`,
`answeredTopics`, `deferredTopics` and `unansweredTopics` are the facts. Deferred
topics are acknowledged gaps: mention them, because they stay open, and never
present them as covered.

### Optional phases

When `nextAction.kind` is `decide-optional-phase`, ask the user and then record the
answer so it survives into later sessions:

```
doc-agent-ai decide-phase --node <node> --phase <phase> --decision <accepted|declined>
```

Ask the question through your platform's interactive option selector, per Global
Agent Rule 13. A declined phase drops out of the workflow and blocks nothing
downstream, and it will not be asked again.

### What you no longer do

- You do not write phase artifacts. The phase sub-agents submit content and the
  program writes it.
- You do not create or update the index, mark checkboxes, or recalculate node
  status. All three are computed from the recorded answers.
- You do not decide that a phase has "enough" information. Coverage is counted, and
  the count is in the status output.
